        <article class="lesson-article">
            <header class="lesson-header">
                <span class="lesson-number">Lesson 1</span>
                <h1>Introduction to EF Core</h1>
                <div class="lesson-meta"><span class="lesson-duration"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>25 min</span><span class="lesson-level intermediate">Intermediate</span></div>
            </header>
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is Entity Framework Core?</h2>
                    <p>Entity Framework Core (EF Core) is a modern, lightweight, extensible, and cross-platform Object-Relational Mapper (ORM) for .NET. It enables developers to work with databases using .NET objects, eliminating most of the data-access code.</p>
                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg><span>Key Benefits</span></div>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Write C# instead of SQL for most operations</li>
                            <li>Type-safe queries with compile-time checking</li>
                            <li>Database-agnostic code (supports SQL Server, PostgreSQL, SQLite, etc.)</li>
                            <li>Automatic change tracking and migration support</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>How EF Core Works</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Your C# code
var products = await context.Products
    .Where(p => p.Price > 100)
    .OrderBy(p => p.Name)
    .ToListAsync();

// EF Core generates SQL
// SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Price > 100 ORDER BY Name</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>EF Core vs EF6 (Legacy)</h2>
                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>EF Core (Modern)</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Cross-platform</li>
                                <li>Lightweight and fast</li>
                                <li>Modular design</li>
                                <li>Better LINQ translation</li>
                                <li>Active development</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>EF6 (Legacy)</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>.NET Framework only</li>
                                <li>Heavier runtime</li>
                                <li>More features (some)</li>
                                <li>Maintenance mode</li>
                                <li>EDMX designer support</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Supported Databases</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>SQL Server</strong> - Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer</li>
                        <li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong> - Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL</li>
                        <li><strong>SQLite</strong> - Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite</li>
                        <li><strong>MySQL</strong> - Pomelo.EntityFrameworkCore.MySql</li>
                        <li><strong>Cosmos DB</strong> - Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Cosmos</li>
                        <li><strong>In-Memory</strong> - Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory (testing)</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Installing EF Core</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Core package
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore

# Database provider (pick one)
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite

# Design-time tools for migrations
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design

# CLI tools (global)
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Core Concepts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>DbContext:</strong> The main class that coordinates EF Core functionality</li>
                        <li><strong>DbSet&lt;T&gt;:</strong> Represents a table in the database</li>
                        <li><strong>Entity:</strong> A class that maps to a database table</li>
                        <li><strong>Migration:</strong> A way to update the database schema</li>
                        <li><strong>Change Tracker:</strong> Tracks modifications to entities</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>EF Core is a modern, cross-platform ORM for .NET.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>It translates LINQ queries to SQL automatically.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Supports multiple database providers.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>DbContext is the heart of EF Core operations.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>
